<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      DELIBERATELY A STANDALONE PROJECT, NOT A MAVEN MODULE.

      There is no <parent> and the root pom.xml has no <modules>. Two reasons:

      1. This has nothing to do with Spring. Inheriting spring-boot-starter-parent
         would drag the Spring BOM, the Spring dependency-management and the
         spring-boot-maven-plugin into a function whose whole selling point is not
         paying for framework initialisation on every cold start.

         The jar this produces measures 10.3 MB at the versions pinned below
         (AWS SDK, Jackson, and 644 KB of Joda-Time that comes with the events
         library — see the exclusion note on aws-lambda-java-events). A Boot fat
         jar would be roughly 45 MB on top of that.

         NO COLD-START FIGURE IS CLAIMED FOR THIS FUNCTION, because none has been
         measured against a deployed version. For orientation only, published
         figures for a plain SDK handler at 1024 MB land around 1-2s, dominated by
         building the DynamoDB client rather than by JVM startup, and Spring Boot
         on Lambda is the 3-6s case. Those are other people's benchmarks. The
         design decisions above (no framework, static client, IODH) follow from
         that shape, not from a number this project produced.

      2. Turning the root into an aggregator pom would break ../Dockerfile, which
         copies a single pom.xml and expects `mvn package` to build one artifact.
         A reactor build needs the parent AND every module's pom present before
         dependency resolution, which is a materially more complicated Dockerfile
         for zero benefit here.

      Consequence to be aware of: nothing manages versions for this pom, so every
      version below is pinned by hand and nothing tells you when they go stale.
      That is the honest trade-off of not having a parent — see the AWS SDK
      comment in ../pom.xml for the same problem in the other direction.

          mvn -f lambda/pom.xml clean package    # -> lambda/target/booking-event-handler.jar
    -->

    <groupId>com.smit</groupId>
    <artifactId>booking-event-handler</artifactId>
    <version>1.1.0</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <name>booking-event-handler</name>
    <description>SQS-triggered Lambda that projects booking events into DynamoDB</description>
    <url>https://github.com/smit-lakhani-13/flight-ops-services</url>
    <inceptionYear>2026</inceptionYear>

    <licenses>
        <license>
            <name>MIT License</name>
            <url>https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT</url>
            <distribution>repo</distribution>
        </license>
    </licenses>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>

        <!--
          `release` rather than `source` + `target`. The difference matters here:
          source/target only control syntax and bytecode level, so compiling with a
          newer JDK will happily link against methods that do not exist in Java 21
          and the failure shows up as a NoSuchMethodError at runtime, in
          production, inside a Lambda. `release` compiles against the Java 21 API
          signatures, so that mistake becomes a compile error instead.

          Not academic on any developer machine with more than one JDK installed:
          a stray JAVA_HOME really can pick the wrong one, and `release` turns
          that into a build failure instead of a runtime one.
        -->
        <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>

        <!--
          Must match `Runtime: java21` in ../template.yaml. If the bytecode is
          newer than the runtime, every invocation dies with
          UnsupportedClassVersionError and CloudWatch shows nothing else.

          Lambda also offers java25 (Corretto 25, added Nov 2025). Staying on 21
          is a deliberate choice, not inertia: 21 is the LTS the main service
          targets, and keeping both modules on one toolchain means one JAVA_HOME
          to get right. Bumping the runtime without bumping
          maven.compiler.release is the UnsupportedClassVersionError above in
          reverse, so the two move together or not at all.
        -->
        <aws.lambda.core.version>1.4.0</aws.lambda.core.version>
        <aws.lambda.events.version>3.16.1</aws.lambda.events.version>
        <aws.lambda.tests.version>1.1.2</aws.lambda.tests.version>

        <!-- Same pin as ../pom.xml. Keep the two in step. -->
        <aws.sdk.version>2.55.2</aws.sdk.version>

        <!--
          Jackson is pinned explicitly because the AWS SDK BOM does NOT manage
          jackson-databind for you — the SDK shades its own copy internally. Rely
          on a transitive Jackson and a future SDK bump can silently change the
          serialisation behaviour of this handler.

          Same version as <jackson-2-bom.version> in the root pom.xml, so the
          producer and this consumer read and write the event contract with one
          Jackson. Move them together; CONTRIBUTING.md says so next to the same
          rule for the AWS SDK BOM.
        -->
        <jackson.version>2.22.2</jackson.version>

        <!--
          Held at whatever major the service module is on, which is the only
          reason this property exists. That used to mean 5.x, because Boot 3.5
          managed JUnit 5; the Boot 4.1 upgrade moved the service to JUnit 6, so
          this moved with it. Two JUnit majors in one repository is a trap when
          you are switching between the modules - the assertion idioms diverge
          and the failure output differs - and the trap does not care which
          module is the one left behind.
        -->
        <junit.version>6.0.3</junit.version>
        <mockito.version>5.23.0</mockito.version>
        <assertj.version>3.27.7</assertj.version>

        <maven.enforcer.plugin.version>3.6.3</maven.enforcer.plugin.version>
        <cyclonedx.plugin.version>2.9.3</cyclonedx.plugin.version>
        <maven.compiler.plugin.version>3.16.0</maven.compiler.plugin.version>
        <maven.surefire.plugin.version>3.6.0</maven.surefire.plugin.version>
        <maven.shade.plugin.version>3.6.2</maven.shade.plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!-- Aligns every software.amazon.awssdk:* artifact to one version. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
                <artifactId>bom</artifactId>
                <version>${aws.sdk.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.junit</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${junit.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Context, RequestHandler, LambdaLogger. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.amazonaws</groupId>
            <artifactId>aws-lambda-java-core</artifactId>
            <version>${aws.lambda.core.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          SQSEvent / SQSBatchResponse. The batch-response type is what makes
          ReportBatchItemFailures work; without it there is nothing to return.

          Brings joda-time 2.10.8 with it: 644 KB, 247 classes, ~6% of the
          deployment package. DO NOT EXCLUDE IT — tried, and it fails.

          The reasoning that says you can is seductive and wrong. Only six event
          types reference Joda (SNSEvent, ScheduledEvent, CodeCommitEvent,
          S3EventNotification, APIGatewayV2CustomAuthorizerEvent,
          LambdaDestinationEvent — verified against the constant pool of all 331
          classes), and SQSEvent is not one of them. But deserialisation does not go
          through SQSEvent's own bytecode: LambdaEventSerializers registers a Jackson
          Joda module for the whole event model, so the class is needed to build the
          serializer at all. Excluding it fails with
            ClassNotFoundException: org.joda.time.DateTime
          from EventLoader — caught by BookingEventHandlerTest, which is the reason
          those two EventLoader-based tests earn their keep.

          Re-adding joda-time at <scope>test</scope> would make the build green
          again and is the trap to avoid: the deployed jar would then differ from the
          tested classpath on exactly the path that just broke, and `sam local
          invoke` is not available here to catch it.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.amazonaws</groupId>
            <artifactId>aws-lambda-java-events</artifactId>
            <version>${aws.lambda.events.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The LOW-LEVEL DynamoDB client, deliberately not dynamodb-enhanced.

          The enhanced client is the nicer API — annotated beans, no
          AttributeValue.fromS() by hand. It also uses reflection over a
          @DynamoDbBean class to build a TableSchema at runtime, which costs cold
          start time and is exactly the kind of thing that breaks under GraalVM
          native-image without extra config. For a handler that writes five
          attributes, the low-level client is smaller, faster to initialise, and
          makes the conditional write completely explicit.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
            <artifactId>dynamodb</artifactId>

            <!--
              THESE EXCLUSIONS ARE LOAD-BEARING. Measured, not assumed.

              Every SDK service module drags in THREE HTTP clients transitively:
              netty-nio-client (async), apache5-client (sync, httpclient5) and
              apache-client (sync, httpclient 4.x). Declaring a fourth one below
              does NOT displace them — Maven has no such mechanism, and believing
              it does was the original mistake here.

              Measured on this project: leaving them in put 2,576 io/netty classes
              and two redundant Apache stacks into an 18 MB uber-jar, for a handler
              that makes one synchronous PutItem call. Excluding them takes it to
              10.3 MB. Every megabyte is code Lambda downloads and unpacks before
              the first invocation.

              The subtler problem is correctness, and it is the real reason for
              this block. Both sync clients register through
              META-INF/services/software.amazon.awssdk.http.SdkHttpService, and
              shade merges those files, so the uber-jar advertises TWO
              implementations and the SDK picks one by classpath order. Verified
              with ServiceLoader against the built jar. SDK 2.54.18 does not throw
              on this — it silently resolves one — which is worse than failing,
              because it means a dependency bump can change how this function talks
              to DynamoDB with no signal at all. Other SDK versions do fail here:
                "Multiple HTTP implementations were found on the classpath"

              Excluding the ASYNC client is safe only because this uses the
              synchronous DynamoDbClient. Switch to DynamoDbAsyncClient and it dies
              at init with "Unable to load an HTTP implementation" — that is the
              trade, and it is deliberate.
            -->
            <exclusions>
                <exclusion>
                    <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
                    <artifactId>netty-nio-client</artifactId>
                </exclusion>
                <exclusion>
                    <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
                    <artifactId>apache5-client</artifactId>
                </exclusion>
                <exclusion>
                    <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
                    <artifactId>apache-client</artifactId>
                </exclusion>
            </exclusions>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The one HTTP client that remains, chosen for Lambda specifically.

          UrlConnectionHttpClient wraps java.net.HttpURLConnection, so the whole
          artifact is 34 KB and pulls in NOTHING. Apache brings ~723 classes of
          httpclient/httpcore/commons-codec with it. In Lambda that difference is
          paid on every cold start, and this function is a single PutItem — there
          is no sustained connection pool to amortise a heavier client over. AWS
          documents this client as the one to prefer for Lambda for exactly that
          reason.

          What you give up, honestly: no HTTP/2, no configurable connection pool
          (HttpURLConnection does its own keep-alive and you cannot tune it), and
          no async support. On a long-lived server handling steady traffic those
          matter and apache5-client is the right answer instead — which is why the
          main service in ../pom.xml does not copy this choice.

          NOT apache-client: software.amazon.awssdk.http.apache.ApacheHttpClient is
          DEPRECATED as of 2.54.18 (javap confirms the annotation). apache5-client
          is its non-deprecated successor. Either would work; neither is worth
          723 classes here.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
            <artifactId>url-connection-client</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
            <version>${jackson.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test scope only from here down. -->

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
            <artifactId>mockito-core</artifactId>
            <version>${mockito.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
            <artifactId>mockito-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>${mockito.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
            <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
            <version>${assertj.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          aws-lambda-java-tests deserialises a real SQS event JSON file into an
          SQSEvent, so the test exercises the same shape Lambda actually delivers
          instead of a hand-built object that might not match. Used by
          BookingEventHandlerTest to load ../events/sqs.json.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.amazonaws</groupId>
            <artifactId>aws-lambda-java-tests</artifactId>
            <version>${aws.lambda.tests.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <!-- Lambda's Handler string names a class, not a jar, so the file name is
             free. A stable name (no version suffix) keeps template.yaml and the
             deploy commands from needing an edit on every version bump. -->
        <finalName>booking-event-handler</finalName>

        <!--
          ../events is on the TEST classpath so BookingEventHandlerTest and
          `sam local invoke -e events/sqs.json` read the SAME fixture files.

          Two copies of a sample event is how a test ends up passing against a
          payload shape that Lambda no longer sends. EventLoader resolves its
          argument through ClassLoader.getResourceAsStream, so copying ../events
          to target/test-classes makes loadSQSEvent("sqs.json") find it.

          Declaring <testResources> at all overrides the default, hence
          src/test/resources has to be listed again explicitly. Maven skips a
          <directory> that does not exist, so the src/test/resources entry costs
          nothing while that directory is empty.
        -->
        <testResources>
            <testResource>
                <directory>src/test/resources</directory>
            </testResource>
            <testResource>
                <directory>../events</directory>
            </testResource>
        </testResources>

        <plugins>
            <!--
              Same toolchain gate as the service module, and it earns its place
              here more than there: this POM has no parent, so nothing else in
              the file checks which JDK is running. maven.compiler.release=21
              above makes a JDK 17 build fail at compilation with a message about
              a release version; this makes it fail at validate with a message
              about the JDK.

              requireUpperBoundDeps is also worth more here. The service module
              has Boot's BOM arbitrating every transitive version; this module
              has three BOMs it imports by hand and a shade plugin that flattens
              whatever they resolve to into one jar.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.enforcer.plugin.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>enforce-toolchain</id>
                        <phase>validate</phase>
                        <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <rules>
                                <requireJavaVersion>
                                    <version>[21,22)</version>
                                    <message>This build needs JDK 21. On this Mac: export JAVA_HOME=/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@21</message>
                                </requireJavaVersion>
                                <requireMavenVersion>
                                    <version>[3.9.0,)</version>
                                    <message>Use ./mvnw -f lambda/pom.xml, which pins the wrapper to a known Maven version.</message>
                                </requireMavenVersion>
                                <banDuplicatePomDependencyVersions/>
                                <requireUpperBoundDeps/>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              An SBOM for the Lambda artifact too. It is the module where it
              matters most: shade inlines every dependency into one jar, so the
              deployed file has no dependency list of its own to read. This is
              that list.

              No JaCoCo here on purpose. This module is one handler class, and
              its tests are written against the behaviours that can actually go
              wrong (duplicate suppression, partial batch failure, sort-key
              stability, a malformed or incomplete payload) rather than
              against a percentage. A coverage gate here would measure the same
              file twice; the service module is where the coverage question is
              real. scripts/numbers.sh prints the count, so it is not repeated
              in prose that would go stale on the next test added.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.cyclonedx</groupId>
                <artifactId>cyclonedx-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${cyclonedx.plugin.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>build-sbom</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals><goal>makeBom</goal></goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
                <configuration>
                    <outputFormat>json</outputFormat>
                    <outputName>bom</outputName>
                    <includeTestScope>false</includeTestScope>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.compiler.plugin.version}</version>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.surefire.plugin.version}</version>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.shade.plugin.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals><goal>shade</goal></goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
                <configuration>
                    <!--
                      Shade rewrites the pom to strip the dependencies it inlined
                      and, by default, installs that rewritten pom. Harmless when
                      nothing depends on this artifact, confusing the moment
                      something does. Off.
                    -->
                    <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>

                    <transformers>
                        <!--
                          THE ONE THAT BREAKS THINGS IF YOU FORGET IT.

                          The AWS SDK finds its HTTP client implementation through
                          META-INF/services/ — the ServiceLoader SPI. Several SDK
                          jars each ship a file at the SAME path, and a plain jar
                          merge is last-one-wins, so all but one silently vanish.
                          The symptom is
                            "Unable to load an HTTP implementation from any
                             provider in the chain"
                          at runtime — never at build time. This transformer
                          concatenates them instead of overwriting.
                        -->
                        <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>

                        <!--
                          Same collision, different mechanism. Multiple SDK
                          modules contribute execution interceptors through this
                          one file; keeping only the last one loses the others.
                          Nothing fails loudly, the interceptors just do not run.
                        -->
                        <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.AppendingTransformer">
                            <resource>software/amazon/awssdk/global/handlers/execution.interceptors</resource>
                        </transformer>
                    </transformers>

                    <filters>
                        <filter>
                            <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                            <excludes>
                                <!--
                                  Signed jars carry digests of their own contents.
                                  Repackaging invalidates them and the JVM refuses
                                  to load the classes:
                                    "Invalid signature file digest for Manifest
                                     main attributes"
                                  Dropping the signature files is the standard fix
                                  and the reason is worth knowing: an uber-jar
                                  cannot preserve a signature it has just broken.
                                -->
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>

                                <!--
                                  A merged uber-jar cannot be a valid JPMS module —
                                  several inlined jars each declare their own
                                  module-info. Lambda runs this on the classpath,
                                  so the descriptors are dead weight at best and a
                                  split-package error at worst.
                                -->
                                <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                            </excludes>
                        </filter>
                    </filters>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
